# 213-DRV-0111 — `display` sleeps between its polls: a light-up no longer holds the CPU the mice share — done

**Type:** driver (`display`) · **Owner:** `DRV` · **Priority:** P2 · **Status:** done (2026-11-26) · **Blocked by:** — · **Main task:** [213](../issues/213-amd-ryzen-pc.md) · **Roadmap:** the drivers track · **Constitution:** MC-12.1, MC-12.2, MC-12.3

## Problem

On the Ryzen the pointer moves in jerks each time the second monitor is detected. The maintainer, 2026-11-26, after
taking the HDMI plug out and putting it back several times on boot 107: «Заметил, что мышь тормозит в момент
определения монитора - движется рывками.»

**What `display` does at a light-up.** Step 3 takes about 2 s (`STEP 3'S TIMES (MS): IN ALL 2028.0; … 9 509.2, … 15
1040.3`). For about 1.5 s of that it holds its CPU without a pause:
- **Stage 9** counts 30 frames of the firmware's timing generator (0.5 s), reading the frame count every 100 us. The
  service's clock waits anything under a millisecond awake (`spin_loop`).
- **Stage 15's watch** reads the frame count for 60 frames (1 s) in a loop with no wait at all.

**Whom that keeps waiting.** A task stays on the CPU it was started on. On boot 107 `ps` shows CPU 6 shared by
`usb_host` (PID 7), `bt_hci` (PID 23) and `display` (PID 39). Both mice pass through those two: the USB receiver's
reports through `usb_host`, the Bluetooth mouse's through `bt_hci`, whose transfers `usb_host` also carries. While
`display` holds the CPU they run only when its time slice ends.

**The kernel's lines for the minutes of four plug changes** (boot 107, minutes 33 and 34; an untouched minute has 2 to
13 waits behind another task):
- minute 33: `58 BEHIND ANOTHER TASK (MOST 64 MS, BEHIND PID 5)`, the longest late wait 81 ms;
- minute 34: `67 BEHIND ANOTHER TASK (MOST 10 MS, BEHIND PID 7)`.

The line names only the task behind the longest wait. PID 5 is the compositor, which held its own CPU for 64 ms once
in minute 33; that is not `display`'s and is told to `KRN`. What share of the jerks each has is not measured.

## Plan

1. Stage 9 waits awake only for its two edges, a frame at most each, and sleeps through the frames between. It counts
   from one edge to another 30 frames later or more, so a wake that comes late only makes the count longer. The edges are
   polled every 20 us in place of 100, as the awake part is now short: the count is more exact than before.
2. The watch sleeps a millisecond when no new frame has come. The underflow and timeout statuses are kept until they
   are cleared, so none is lost; a wake later than a frame shows in the line's "passed between two reads" and in its
   longest gap, as a task kept from running already did.
3. The plan's status waits (`Step::Wait`, polled every 100 us) stay awake: some wait for a live bit that a sparse poll
   could miss. On the Ryzen they are 82 to 94 ms of a light-up, in pieces of 42 ms at most.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Stage 9 is awake for two frames at most and asleep for the rest of its count; a wake 40 ms late gives a longer
  count with the same pixel clock and reference clock (`display_light_host.rs`,
  `stage_9_and_the_watch_sleep_between_their_polls_and_a_late_wake_spoils_neither`).
- [x] The watch sleeps between its looks and reads every frame when woken on time; a wake 40 ms late marks the frames
  slept through and names the gap; an underflow during such a sleep is found at the next look (the same case). With the
  polls awake again the case fails.
- [x] The other cases of `display_light_host.rs` pass unchanged, the one on boot 70's values among them (its sync
  values depend on the count to 0.01 %).
- [x] On the Ryzen: the second output lights in its monitor's mode with the same lines as before, the plug taken out
  and put back while the mouse is moved, and the maintainer's look at the pointer (MC-12.1). Boot 108, below.
- [x] The kernel's minute line for that minute, against minutes 33 and 34 above. Boot 108, below.

## Related

[213-DRV-0095](213-DRV-0095-display-the-second-outputs-mode-from-its-edid.done) (the light-up this
changes), [213-DRV-0103](213-DRV-0103-display-a-standing-underflow-check.done) (the standing check, which
already sleeps), 000-KRN-0276 and 000-KRN-0283 (late wakes on an idle CPU: what makes a millisecond's sleep longer).

## Resolution

Done on 2026-11-26. At a light-up `display` now waits awake only for stage 9's two frame edges and sleeps between its
polls in stage 9's count and stage 15's watch.

**On the Ryzen** (release 202611262015, boot 108: `claude/DRV-ryzen-bench-42-hdmi2` abe47faa4, which is `main`
9d6feac696 with this change, 213-DRV-0112's read-only reads and a bench-only line of `init`). It holds for that machine,
that monitor and that release (MC-12.1). Through QEMU before it: "x86: keys, shell, tools", "x86: services, storage,
audio" and the update from the release before.

- **The second output lit as before**, at boot and after each of two plug changes by the maintainer (lit again at
  21:12:28 and 21:13:21 UTC), in the monitor's mode, 3440x1440:
  - `STEP 3'S TIMES (MS): IN ALL 2015.0; … 9 500.4, … 15 1041.1` and `IN ALL 2017.0; … 9 507.9, … 15 1042.0`; before
    the change 2011.0 to 2030.4.
  - `S15 UNBLANK DONE; … WATCHED 60 FRAMES IN 1001 MS CLEAN, LONGEST GAP 3093 US AT FRAME 20`, and `… IN 1000 MS CLEAN,
    LONGEST GAP 2558 US AT FRAME 27`. Before the change the two such lines read had 1119 and 1263 us: the watch now
    sleeps a millisecond between its looks, and no frame passed between two reads.
  - `S9 REFERENCE DONE; … OTG0 AT 268500 KHZ` at boot and `268505` after the second plug change; before the change
    268476 to 268535 over six light-ups.
- **The maintainer's look**, the mouse moved while the plug went out and in: «рывков нет при перетыкании». Of the old
  service on boot 107 he had said that the pointer moved in jerks while the monitor was detected, and later that it
  lagged a little.
- **The kernel's minute lines** (`logs 1`, read every minute):

  | | Plug changes in the minute | Behind another task | The longest, and behind whom |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | boot 107, minute 25, the old service | 1 | 28 | 11 ms, PID 21 |
  | boot 107, minute 33 | 2 | 58 | 64 ms, PID 5 (the compositor) |
  | boot 107, minute 34 | 2 | 67 | 10 ms, PID 7 (`usb_host`) |
  | boot 107, minute 51 | 1 | 44 | 15 ms, PID 7 |
  | **boot 108, minute 53, this change** | 1 | **18** | 15 ms, PID 7 |
  | **boot 108, minute 54** | 1 | **8** | 8 ms, PID 7 |
  | boot 108, the 53 minutes before | none | 1 to 16 | 3 to 16 ms |

**What is not shown.**
- How much of the jerks was `display`'s and how much the compositor's 64 ms on its own CPU (boot 107, minute 33): the
  compositor's part is told to `KRN` and was not seen again in these two minutes, which proves nothing about it.
- A machine where `display` shares its CPU with other services than these.
- The plan's status waits are still awake: 78 to 82 ms of a light-up here, in pieces of 42 ms at most.
